ReplayGain for wave files, v1.4.2

WaveGain is an application of the ReplayGain algorithms to standard PCM wave
files. Calculated gain adjustments are applied directly to the audio data,
instead of just writing metadata as traditionally done for other formats
like MP3, FLAC and Ogg Vorbis.

The replaygain values can also be added as metadata in a custom RIFF chunk
named 'gain'. This could theoretically allow WAV files to have same lossless
functionality as other formats where audio data is not altered. But since no
current players are aware of this "standard", the metadata is used only by
WaveGain for the "--undo-gain" feature, which is lossy.


About replaygain
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Replaygain is a standard that allows compatible players to play audio files with
an adjusted volume gain so they have a consistent perceived loudness.

It is similar in concept to Normalization, but instead of using just the peak
value to calculate the gain, it relies on RMS (sound pressure "power") values
and psychoacoustics models to calculate a gain based on the perceived loudness
of an audio file by a human listener.

Loudness can be averaged either for each individual file, also called "Track" or
"Radio" mode, where every song is adjusted to the same standard loudness; or by
group, also called "Album" mode, where individual "songs" may sound louder or
quieter than others, but each "album" as a whole has the same average loudness
as other albums.

For more information,
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ReplayGain
http://wiki.hydrogenaudio.org/index.php?title=Replaygain
http://wiki.hydrogenaudio.org/index.php?title=ReplayGain_specification


The saga of a Debian package for wavegain:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Current source code (in .zip format) and windows binaries are published at
	http://www.rarewares.org/others.php
	http://www.rarewares.org/files/others/

- An i386 binary .deb for version 1.2.6 (from 2005) was published at
	http://www.rarewares.org/debian/packages/unstable/index.php

- Later, Linux Mint hosted the source, amd64 and i386 packages for v1.2.8 (2010)
	http://packages.linuxmint.com/pool/import/w/wavegain/

This package is a combination of current upstream source, a proper Debian
packaging improved from the Mint release, and some patches to fix some
(serious) Linux issues.


Install how-to
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There are 2 approaches: you can build and install a .deb package, or simply
compile and install from source. For both I assume you are already at the
source directory (which is not the root directory of the cloned repository)

Debian method:
This is OUTDATED, UNMAINTANED and NON-WORKING! Use Traditional Method instead!
$ sudo apt-get install devscripts # if you don't already have debuild
$ debuild -- binary && sudo dpkg --install ../wavegain_*.deb

Traditional method:
$ make && sudo make install # by default installs to /usr/local/bin/wavegain
or
$ make && sudo make install prefix=/usr # to install to /usr/bin/wavegain

The default build uses the compiler's native architecture (including 64-bit).
PCM samples use fixed-width 32-bit storage, and POSIX builds enable 64-bit file
offsets, so WAV files larger than 2 GiB and 4 GiB are processed in full.
WAV files whose sizes are 0xFFFFFFFF (written to a pipe, or "oversized" WAV
over 4 GiB) are read to the end of the file. RF64 input is supported.
Sony Wave64 (.w64) input and output are also supported, with 64-bit chunk
sizes and sample counts. Wave64 input is detected by its GUIDs and remains
Wave64 when gain is applied, including when -b changes the PCM format.

To explicitly request a 32-bit build on an x86 toolchain, use:

    make CFLAGS=-m32

This may require multilib headers and libraries, e.g. gcc-multilib on Debian.
Run the PCM and large-file regression tests with `make check`.

Wave64 examples:

    wavegain recording.w64             # calculate gain
    wavegain -y recording.w64          # apply gain in the same container
    wavegain -y -w recording.w64       # also store the applied gain
    wavegain --undo-gain recording.w64 # reverse the stored gain (lossy)
    wavegain -y -b 3 recording.w64     # convert samples to 24-bit PCM

Wave64 supports the same mono/stereo PCM and sample rates as WAV. Unknown
chunks before and after audio are preserved with bounded-memory copying.
'fact' and gain chunks are rewritten before the audio, and 'levl' peak
summaries (stale after a gain change) are dropped, wherever they appear.
On seekable input, container or data sizes that were never finalized (for
example a file written to a pipe) or that exceed a truncated file are
accepted with a warning, like the WAV reader; stdin input stays strict.
The WaveGain gain extension uses a private GUID
(e217d848-96be-4ac0-b56a-8b46289c71e0), containing a little-endian IEEE double.
Files must contain a format chunk before a single audio data chunk; compressed
codecs remain unsupported. -b 6 explicitly selects AIFF output and
retains that format's 32-bit size limit. No WAV-to-Wave64 conversion option is
provided; use an external converter to create the initial .w64 file.

WAV output over 4 GiB, which RIFF's 32-bit sizes cannot describe, is written
as before with its RIFF and data sizes set to 0xFFFFFFFF (an "oversized" WAV
that most tools read to the end of the file), with a warning. With --rf64 such
output is written as EBU RF64 instead: a JUNK chunk is reserved after the
header and becomes the ds64 chunk with 64-bit sizes only when the output
exceeds 4 GiB, so smaller output stays a plain RIFF/WAV file. --rf64 needs a
seekable output and is ignored with --stdout. RF64 input is rewritten the same
way, so it stays RF64 when it is still over 4 GiB.

`make check` includes sparse 5 GiB Wave64 input/output, padding, metadata,
PCM/float, extensible format, gain and malformed-size regressions. Optional
FFmpeg interoperability checks can be run with `make check-interop`.

`make verified-release` builds a release binary and compares the ReplayGain
filter state and complete conversion output against a scalar build made with
the same compiler and flags, deleting the binary on any mismatch.
VERIFY_SIMD=auto (default) accepts either filter backend, VERIFY_SIMD=1
requires the paired stereo SIMD backend and VERIFY_SIMD=0 requires scalar.


Behavior changes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Results can differ from earlier releases for the same input:

* Clipping prevention and the reported Peak use the peak AFTER the selected
  DC offset correction (track or album), instead of the uncorrected peak.
* DC offset correction is applied when the offset of either channel exceeds
  1/32768 of full scale. Earlier releases required at least 2/32768.
* With -t (--limiter) every file is rewritten, even when no gain change or
  DC correction would otherwise be needed.
* 88.2 kHz files use new filter coefficients (the former ones were unstable),
  so their gain values change. Other rates can also change on FMA-capable
  builds (next item). MSVC builds using the legacy SSE2 filter (HAVE_SSE2)
  previously ignored the last Yule coefficient, so they change at every rate.
* The ReplayGain filters are evaluated without fused multiply-add (FMA). On
  FMA-capable builds (AArch64, or x86_64 with -march=x86-64-v3 or later) the
  filter rounding differs from earlier builds that let the compiler fuse
  operations, so at any sample rate a reported gain close to a 0.01 dB
  histogram step can move by one step (e.g. -0.44 dB -> -0.45 dB at 48 kHz).
  Builds that cannot use FMA (e.g. the default x86_64 -O2) give the same
  results as before. Within one build the scalar and SIMD filters match
  bit-for-bit; gain values matched across the compilers and targets tested
  for this change, but RMS accumulation and log10 are outside this rule, so
  identical gains across all compilers and architectures are not guaranteed.

